From: Herman van Hazendonk <github.com@herrie.org>
Date: Sun, 11 Oct 2026 05:59:11 +0200
Subject: [PATCH] Halium: let a device opt out of the low 4GB reservation

PartitionAlloc reserves the first 4GB + 1MB of a 64-bit Linux process as an
inaccessible "zero segment" (enable_user_space_zero_segment, on by default
for 64-bit Linux), and V8's sandbox reserves the same range itself when
PartitionAlloc has not. Both are hardening against Smi/pointer confusion.

The 3.10 kgsl driver of the Adreno 4xx (Nexus 5X, msm8992) can only map a
GPU buffer that the CPU also maps at an address below 0xc0000000, because the
GPU addresses 32 bits. With the whole range reserved, every such mapping in
the process fails with -ENOMEM, EGL never initialises and the in-process GPU
thread of WebAppMgr and the browser crashes. /proc/<pid>/maps of WebAppMgr
shows the reservation as "00010000-100100000 ---p ... [anon:partition_alloc]".

Skip both reservations when /etc/webruntime/no-low-4gb-reservation exists,
so only the device that needs it installs that file and every other device
keeps the hardening.

Signed-off-by: Herman van Hazendonk <github.com@herrie.org>
---
Upstream-Status: Inappropriate [Halium specific]
---

diff --git a/src/base/allocator/partition_allocator/src/partition_alloc/partition_address_space.cc b/src/base/allocator/partition_allocator/src/partition_alloc/partition_address_space.cc
--- a/src/base/allocator/partition_allocator/src/partition_alloc/partition_address_space.cc
+++ b/src/base/allocator/partition_allocator/src/partition_alloc/partition_address_space.cc
@@ -39,6 +39,10 @@
 #include <sys/mman.h>
 #endif
 
+#if PA_CONFIG(ENABLE_USER_SPACE_ZERO_SEGMENT) && PA_BUILDFLAG(IS_LINUX)
+#include <unistd.h>
+#endif
+
 namespace partition_alloc::internal {
 
 #if PA_BUILDFLAG(HAS_64_BIT_POINTERS)
@@ -251,6 +255,17 @@
     return;
   }
 
+#if PA_BUILDFLAG(IS_LINUX)
+  // Some GPU drivers can only map buffers into the low 4GB: the 3.10 kgsl
+  // driver of the Adreno 4xx needs CPU addresses below 0xc0000000 because the
+  // GPU addresses 32 bits. Reserving that range makes every GPU buffer
+  // allocation fail there, so a device can opt out with a marker file. V8
+  // checks the same file before it reserves the range itself.
+  if (access("/etc/webruntime/no-low-4gb-reservation", F_OK) == 0) {
+    return;
+  }
+#endif
+
   const size_t min_zero_segment_size =
       static_cast<size_t>(PA_CONFIG(USER_SPACE_ZERO_SEGMENT_SIZE_MB)) * 1024 *
       1024;
diff --git a/src/v8/src/sandbox/sandbox.cc b/src/v8/src/sandbox/sandbox.cc
--- a/src/v8/src/sandbox/sandbox.cc
+++ b/src/v8/src/sandbox/sandbox.cc
@@ -24,6 +24,7 @@
 
 #if V8_OS_LINUX
 #include <sys/mman.h>
+#include <unistd.h>
 #endif
 
 namespace v8 {
@@ -309,7 +310,16 @@
   // Also try to reserve the first 4GB of the process' address space. This
   // mitigates Smi<->HeapObject confusion bugs in which we end up treating a
   // Smi value as a pointer.
-  if (!smi_address_range_reserved_) {
+  bool reserve_smi_address_range = true;
+#if V8_OS_LINUX
+  // A device whose GPU driver can only map buffers below 4GB opts out of this
+  // reservation with the same marker file PartitionAlloc checks before it
+  // reserves its zero segment.
+  if (access("/etc/webruntime/no-low-4gb-reservation", F_OK) == 0) {
+    reserve_smi_address_range = false;
+  }
+#endif
+  if (reserve_smi_address_range && !smi_address_range_reserved_) {
     // Make the guard region extend a little past the first 4GB to also catch
     // accesses to in-object properties which are bounded by the JSObject
     // instance size.
